A housing cooperative has sent you three folders of meeting records. You want the latest agenda to print, but nobody remembers which folder holds it. Opening every document would answer a different question: first you simply need a list of plausible filenames. \cmd|find| can make that list while leaving all the records untouched.

\begin{objectives}
\item Search below a known starting path and recognize the paths printed in the result.
\item Pass a quoted name pattern to \texttt{find} rather than asking the shell to expand it.
\item Combine tests for a name and a file type, including dot-prefixed names.
\item Distinguish an empty result from an incomplete or failed search.
\end{objectives}

\input{figures/chapter-9/search.tikz}
\section{Draw a boundary before you search}
\IndexCommand{find}
Choose a writable parent directory and check \cmd|pwd|. Make the new practice root first and stop if it fails; don't continue in a directory that already existed. Replace only the practice root name if necessary. Unlike the cooperative's real folders, this tree contains only short names and empty regular files.

\begin{terminal}
mkdir ./utp-09-records
cd ./utp-09-records
pwd
mkdir -p ./records/april ./records/may ./records/.drafts
touch ./records/april/agenda.txt ./records/may/agenda.txt
touch './records/may/Final Agenda.txt' ./records/.drafts/agenda.txt
mkdir ./records/may/agenda-old.txt
\end{terminal}

The command \cmd|touch| creates an empty file at a new path; given an existing file, it can change timestamps instead. It is suitable for our freshly made fixture, not a way to inspect someone else's files. The last line deliberately makes a directory with a filename-like ending. That distinction will matter.

Start with a short read-only walk. The starting directory is itself one of the paths visited. Explicit \cmd|-print| asks for each path that passes the tests; there are no tests yet, so it prints all paths reached. The word after \cmd|find| is an address, not a keyword. If you change the working directory, the same relative address can point somewhere else.

\begin{terminal}
find ./records -print
\end{terminal}

Compare the names in the result against Figure~\ref{fig:9-boundary}. Your output order can differ. \cmd|find| visits directory entries in an order you shouldn't depend on; the most recently printed path is not necessarily the newest file. The output is useful because it preserves paths: three copies of \cmd|agenda.txt| in different folders can be picked out by the parents that precede the final name.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\UTPNineBoundary
\caption{A starting path names the branch to visit, not the whole computer.}
\label{fig:9-boundary}
\VisualDescription{From the practice root, the walk descends from records into April, May, and dot-drafts. The practice root itself is outside this walk.}
\end{figure}

An explicit starting path is a safety and relevance choice. \cmd|find . -print| is fine when you're already at the confirmed root of a small project. It is not interchangeable with \cmd|find ./records -print| if sibling directories exist. Starting at the filesystem root can visit huge trees and encounter unreadable directories, external storage, or unrelated private filenames. Start with the smallest directory likely to contain what you want; widen the scope only when you can explain why. If real output is too voluminous, interrupt with \KeyChord{Ctrl}{C}; it is a read-only example, so interrupting it stops the search rather than undoing file work.

\section{Match the last component}
\IndexTerm{filename pattern}
The \cmd|-name| test compares just the last path component against a pattern. To seek an exact spelling, give that spelling without a wildcard:

\begin{terminal}
find ./records -name 'agenda.txt' -print
\end{terminal}

There should be three results: one each in April, May, and \cmd|.drafts|. The quotes don't make the search insensitive to case. In particular, \cmd|Final Agenda.txt| is not a match because the name is different. Notice how the hidden parent still appears in an output path: \cmd|find| walks into directories whose names begin with a dot, rather than adopting the default visibility of \cmd|ls|. A search over real work could therefore report names a file manager is set to conceal. Treat the paths as potentially private when sharing the results.

For filenames ending in \cmd|.txt|, put an asterisk before the suffix. In this use the asterisk means any sequence of characters, including no characters. It does not cross directories here because \cmd|-name| compares one final component at a time. This test doesn't examine a file's bytes, so a directory whose name ends in \cmd|.txt| can pass too. Compare these two searches:

\begin{terminal}
find ./records -name '*.txt' -print
find ./records -type f -name '*.txt' -print
\end{terminal}

The first command lists four regular files and one directory. The second lists four regular files; \cmd|-type f| requires a regular file. Adjacent tests in these examples mean both must pass. \cmd|-print| only runs for the paths that pass everything before it. Chapter~\ref{ch:14} develops patterns further; here the working rule is to describe the path's last name and type separately. You could also use \cmd|-type d| for a directory or \cmd|-type l| for a symbolic link under the ordinary traversal used in this chapter.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\UTPNineTests
\caption{Walk first, then test each path that was reached.}
\label{fig:9-tests}
\VisualDescription{A visited path goes through the file-type test and then the name test. Passing both leads to printing; other paths are not printed.}
\end{figure}

As Figure~\ref{fig:9-tests} suggests, matching and traversal answer separate questions. \cmd|-type f| doesn't tell \cmd|find| to ignore all directories; it still needs to walk into directories in order to find files. For instance this next directory-only query reports a directory, but does not change anything about its contents:

\begin{terminal}
find ./records -type d -name '*.txt' -print
find ./records -type d -name '.*' -print
\end{terminal}

The first result is \cmd|./records/may/agenda-old.txt|; the second is \cmd|./records/.drafts|. Add quotation marks around names that contain spaces as well as patterns containing an asterisk. For the title-case name in May:

\begin{terminal}
find ./records -type f -name 'Final Agenda.txt' -print
\end{terminal}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.3in}Y Y}
\toprule
Element & What it controls & What it does not control\\
\midrule
./records & Where the walk begins. & Which reached files are printed.\\
-name 'agenda.txt' & Whether the last component matches. & Whether the file contains a particular word.\\
-type f & Whether the reached object is a regular file. & Whether directories are traversed.\\
-print & Whether a selected path is displayed. & Whether a file is copied, changed, or removed.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Keep the star for the right reader}
Before \cmd|find| sees any words, your shell reads the command line. An unquoted \cmd|*.txt| can match names in the working directory and be turned into literal operands. Some shells stop with an error if nothing matches; others can leave it untouched or remove it under particular settings. None of these outcomes is the intended input. With \cmd|'*.txt'|, the shell strips the quotes and passes the five literal characters inside, including the asterisk. \cmd|find| interprets that asterisk separately for each name it encounters in the search. Figure~\ref{fig:9-quotes} compares the two routes.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\UTPNineQuotes
\caption{Quoting decides which reader gets the asterisk.}
\label{fig:9-quotes}
\VisualDescription{With quotes the asterisk reaches find. Without quotes the shell may replace the pattern before find runs.}
\end{figure}

Here's a way to show the difference without asking \cmd|find| to process ill-formed arguments. Create exactly one top-level sample with a \cmd|.txt| ending. \cmd|printf| uses its format once for the single argument in each line. The first line of output should be \cmd|*.txt| and the second \cmd|shell-sample.txt|:

\begin{terminal}
touch ./shell-sample.txt
printf '%s\n' '*.txt'
printf '%s\n' *.txt
find ./records -type f -name '*.txt' -print
\end{terminal}

The final search still reports the four intended files and never reports the top-level sample because the sample is outside \cmd|./records|. This is a useful diagnostic pattern: if a search works in one directory and fails in another, check whether what you passed was a fixed search expression or an unquoted pattern the shell expanded before the search.

\section{Inspect a result before deciding what to do}
Suppose you want an exact name that is not present. \cmd|find| ordinarily succeeds when it searches successfully and finds no matches. The status reports whether the walk encountered errors, not how many names passed. Compare two safe queries:

\begin{terminal}
find ./records -type f -name 'agenda-final.txt' -print
printf '%s\n' "$?"
find ./missing-records -print
printf '%s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

The first search returns zero with no matching path; the second issues a diagnostic and returns a nonzero status because the starting path is missing. A real search can print some paths and report an error for a different branch. In that case, the printed paths might be valid, but you don't have a complete result. In an apparently successful search without matches, reconsider the starting path, spelling, case, file type, and whether the expected branch is actually a symbolic link. By default the basic walk shown here does not descend through symlinked directories. Avoid reaching for an option that follows every link until you know which branches that would add.

\begin{quicktable}{Y Y}
\toprule
Observation & Reasonable next check\\
\midrule
Zero lines and status zero & Look for spelling or case differences and check the starting path.\\
Some paths and an error & Treat the result as incomplete; read the error and identify the affected branch.\\
Several plausible paths & Read the parent directories or inspect the individual candidates before choosing.\\
A strange or broken-looking printed name & Do not paste it as an unquoted command argument; inspect it with a safe viewer.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

Real names can contain spaces, tabs, and even line breaks. A display with one path apparently on each line is not a dependable machine-readable list for arbitrary input. In this chapter you make a decision interactively from short controlled names; don't pass the displayed output directly to a tool that changes or removes files. Even in a small project, quote a name as one operand and recheck it at the time of action. If a file seems to have vanished since the result was printed, the directory may have changed while you were looking.

\begin{dialoguebox}{Does nothing mean nothing?}
\Brian{My search printed nothing. Can I tell everyone there was no agenda?}
\Stephen{Only if your search covered the place and spelling they meant.}
\Brian{I gave it the right folder. Isn't that enough?}
\Stephen{It could be a different name, or the walk might have reported an unreadable branch.}
\Brian{So a successful walk isn't necessarily a successful guess?}
\Stephen{Exactly. Check the errors first, then try a broader name pattern inside the same branch.}
\DialogueConclusion{No match is not proof of absence.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Practice and clean up}
The three searches below do not change files. To remove the fixture afterward, confirm the working directory with \cmd|pwd|. Use \cmd|rm -i| one path at a time for the four exact regular-file names in \cmd|records| and \cmd|./shell-sample.txt| in the root. This is permanent removal; don't use a pattern. Then use \cmd|rmdir| on empty \cmd|./records/may/agenda-old.txt|, the three empty child directories of \cmd|records|, and \cmd|./records|, in that order. Leave with \cmd|cd ..| and remove the empty practice root using its exact name. If a directory refuses removal, inspect it; it's fine to leave it rather than delete an unexpected item.

\begin{exercise}{Which agendas really are files?}
\label{ex:9-agendas}
Print just regular files with the exact lowercase name \texttt{agenda.txt}. Explain what limits the results and why a hidden parent is present. There is no cleanup for this read-only exercise.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
find ./records -type f -name 'agenda.txt' -print
\end{terminal}
Expect three paths, including one in \texttt{.drafts}. The name test selects the last component, not the parent; traversal includes dot-prefixed entries.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{A scope you can explain}
\label{ex:9-scope}
List the regular text-named files below May, but not below April. No new files need cleanup. Explain why the directory whose name ends in \texttt{.txt} isn't printed.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
find ./records/may -type f -name '*.txt' -print
\end{terminal}
Expect the two regular files in May. The old-agenda directory can pass the name test but fails the file-type test.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Separate absence from failure}
\label{ex:9-absence}
Search for \texttt{minutes.txt} inside the fixture, then run a search with a nonexistent starting path. Check each status immediately. No changes are made.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
find ./records -name 'minutes.txt' -print
printf '%s\n' "$?"
find ./not-here -name 'minutes.txt' -print
printf '%s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}
The first is quiet and successful. The second issues an error and reports a nonzero result. Neither action establishes what could exist outside the starting path.
\end{solution}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Choose the starting path before choosing a pattern.
\item Quote patterns for \texttt{find}; distinguish the last component from the whole path.
\item A successful read-only walk can return no match, and an unsuccessful one can return some.
\end{takeaways}

